From: Stained Glass OS contributors
Subject: crypt32: a standard user trusts the machine's root certificates

A user's Root store is a collection of the user's own and the machine's.
crypt32 opened the machine's for writing unless the caller asked for
read-only, and a standard user may not write HKLM: the open failed and the
machine's roots were silently left out. Every HTTPS server was then
untrusted for that user -- wininet 12045 or 12057 -- so Get a web browser,
installers' downloads and the first-run browser install all failed in a
standard user's session, while the same requests worked as the prefix's
owner and in every single-user test. The machine's store is now opened
read-only when writing is refused, as a Windows user reads it.

Gate: test/userroots-gate.sh (make test-userroots).
---
--- a/dlls/crypt32/store.c
+++ b/dlls/crypt32/store.c
@@ -591,6 +591,17 @@
                 dwFlags |= CERT_SYSTEM_STORE_LOCAL_MACHINE;
                 regStore = CertOpenStore(CERT_STORE_PROV_SYSTEM_REGISTRY_W, 0,
                  0, dwFlags, pvPara);
+                /* Stained Glass OS: a standard user may not write the
+                 * machine's stores; opening them for writing failed, and the
+                 * user's Root store then lacked every machine root -- every
+                 * HTTPS server was untrusted (wininet 12045/12057). The
+                 * user reads them, as on Windows. */
+                if (!regStore && !(dwFlags & CERT_STORE_READONLY_FLAG))
+                {
+                    dwFlags |= CERT_STORE_READONLY_FLAG;
+                    regStore = CertOpenStore(CERT_STORE_PROV_SYSTEM_REGISTRY_W, 0,
+                     0, dwFlags, pvPara);
+                }
                 if (regStore)
                 {
                     CertAddStoreToCollection(store, regStore,
